# Basic Scene Setup

This note is derived from the Three.js snippet set and explains the core idea behind **Basic Scene Setup**.

## Why it matters
Minimal code to create a Three.js scene, camera, and renderer.

## Example
```threejs
import * as THREE from 'three';

const scene = new THREE.Scene();
const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(75, window.innerWidth / window.innerHeight, 0.1, 1000);
const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer();

renderer.setSize(window.innerWidth, window.innerHeight);
document.body.appendChild(renderer.domElement);

camera.position.z = 5;
```

## Review checklist
- Identify the input and output.
- Decide what should be configurable.
- Confirm the naming still matches the domain.
- Add tests or manual checks for failure cases.

# Animated Rotating Cube

This note is derived from the Three.js snippet set and explains the core idea behind **Animated Rotating Cube**.

## Why it matters
Creating a mesh and animating its rotation in a loop.

## Example
```threejs
const geometry = new THREE.BoxGeometry(1, 1, 1);
const material = new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ color: 0x00ff00 });
const cube = new THREE.Mesh(geometry, material);
scene.add(cube);

function animate() {
  requestAnimationFrame(animate);
  cube.rotation.x += 0.01;
  cube.rotation.y += 0.01;
  renderer.render(scene, camera);
}
animate();
```

## Review checklist
- Identify the input and output.
- Decide what should be configurable.
- Confirm the naming still matches the domain.
- Add tests or manual checks for failure cases.

